# Parameters and Arguments

This is VERY important.

You wrote:

```ruby id="1u6d8g"
def number_doubled(number)
```

`number` is the parameter (placeholder).

Then:

```ruby id="qcfj0i"
number_doubled(5)
```

`5` is the argument (actual value passed in).

This matters because real applications constantly receive dynamic data.

Example:

```ruby id="gh0e6x"
def greet(name)
  return "Hello #{name}"
end
```

Now the method works for ANY name:

```ruby id="4g1c9p"
greet("Ashoor")
greet("Michael")
greet("Sarah")
```

That’s reusable programming.

